-### Ingress Handoff And Discriminators
-
-The ingress actor returns one record in its existing ticket/design carrier:
-exact contract/candidate identity; producer/consumer and authority ownership;
-role/frame and source routes; selected corpus-access/runtime dependencies;
-test evidence; compatibility exclusions and remaining obligations. It covers
-install/bind operation identity and the eleven Public-start authority values.
-The scheduled integration review checks those joins and the following cases.
-Executive records its disposition here; Jim rules only on reserved or changed
-Product choices. No additional document family or review round is required.
-
-- **Generic binding:** one minimal non-STDO constitutional-corpus fixture uses
-  the same binding/admission/assembly path without changing generic engine
-  code or a release allowlist. Exact identity/digest mismatches refuse before
-  dependent dispatch. STDO-specific interpretation stays in its declared
-  template/provider boundary. This is a genericity test, not another supported
-  constitution Product or new interpreter.
-- **Proportionate context:** under instruction-assembly requirement 006, add
-  unrelated corpus/workspace material while keeping the selected dependencies
-  fixed: it does not expand the actor context. Selected necessary material
-  remains supplied or accessible; missing/stale required support refuses.
-  Replay reconstructs the inclusion/omission choices. No arbitrary truncation
-  or target percentage substitutes for dependency sufficiency; the measured
-  82% predecessor share alone proves neither waste nor adequacy.
-- **Runtime dependency:** the selected access contract states whether Python
-  executes at runtime, its exact supported executable/capability basis and
-  acquisition route, or the existing lawful alternative. An available selected
-  capability succeeds; missing capability refuses before dependent work.
-  Generic GTL/HoG/ABG acquire no ambient Python or STDO assumption. This decision
-  does not select an a_c port, second access implementation or packaging project.
